From 351fd90c959771ca978df4a148cf7865e4dde16c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:54:22 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/18] NativeLayerWayland: decide, repack and declare stereo
 pictures

Stereo KWin takes two views full side by side with the left eye first,
declared once on a surface (kde_stereo_content_v1 through
libstereo-declare), and shows each eye its half of the surface's source
box over the surface's place.

A picture's arrangement is decided in this order: the stream's frame
packing SEI, the container's mark, the user's choice, all read by the
browser; for a video without any of them, libstereo-layer's picture
policy through its C entry, stereo_layer_video_layout(), loaded at run
time (side by side when the picture is at least three times as wide as
high). The browser keeps no copy of that policy, and Qt is no longer
loaded through a wrapper beside it.

A mark that comes with the size of a view (Matroska with DisplayWidth and
DisplayHeight, a WebXR session) shows each view over the element's place.
A mark without it names the arrangement, not the size of the views: a
picture shown at least 2.5 times as wide as high (side by side) or at
most 1.2 (top and bottom) holds two full views, and one view is then
shown at its own shape in the middle of the element's place.

A picture held side by side with the left eye first and visible across
its whole width is shown as it is; a vertical clip cuts both views alike
through the viewport's source. Top and bottom pictures, right eye first
pictures, and pictures clipped on a side are copied on the GPU, the
visible part of each view into its half of a buffer from the
compositor's pool (GLBlitHelper::Blit takes the part of the surface to
draw), which is then attached and declared. The copy is 8 bit RGB, so
the surface's YCbCr colour representation is reset while it is shown;
HDR video is not repacked yet. Other arrangements stay flat.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
